00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:05.840
updates as a leading indicator for hyperscale CAPEX cycles. While we are thinking about who can

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:12.400
handle higher costs, let's zoom out to a bigger structural question. Imagine you bought a broad market

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:18.520
ETF last week when oil headlines pushed energy names higher. Your order filled at the prevailing

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:25.120
ask, but the ETFs underlying index weights had already shifted another 0.4% toward the largest

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.700
constituents by the time the trade settled. That 0.4% drift is the mechanical outcome of market

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:37.200
cap waiting, where every new passive dollar automatically buys more of the stocks that have already

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:43.900
risen. Over the past decade, this loop has added roughly 1.8 percentage points of annual return to

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:50.980
the S&amp;P 500's largest names, simply from inflows. What most retail investors do not realize is that

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:57.340
active managers still execute the marginal trade that sets closing prices. The concrete misconception

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:04.500
to avoid is assuming if the index keeps rising my ETF will keep working. Instead, always check the

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:10.060
percentage of total trading volume still coming from active desks before adding fresh passive exposure

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:16.420
after a 20% run up in mega caps. That passive flow dynamic matters even more when we look at the

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:23.820
